/* book exam traps: Putting mole fractions y into ∫dY/(Y − Y*) instead of mole ratios Y. That changes both the driving force and the integration variable; for a rich gas the bed comes out wrong by a wide margin. | Using the arithmetic mean of the end driving forces. For 0.01762 and 0.00503 it is 0.0113 against the correct 0.01004 — about 13 % too much driving force, so NOG reads 4.20 instead of 4.74. | Forgetting the 1.2 m distributor and sump allowance, or adding it before multiplying HOG by NOG. | Quoting HG as HOG. HOG = HG + λHL is larger than HG unless the liquid resistance is negligible. | Taking λ = mG/L and A = L/(mG) as the same number, or using total gas flow G instead of the inert flux GM in HOG. */
